WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.032
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:13.930
Hello, everyone, and thanks for
attending the first ever Google

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.220
Genomics webinar.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.820
My name is Sri, I'm the Business
Head for Google Genomics,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.570
and today I'll be
walking you through some

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.620
of the key features, benefits,
and some cool example

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.559
applications of the
genomics platform

00:00:24.559 --> 00:00:26.100
that we are very
actively developing.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.540
It is fairly common knowledge
now that sequencing cost has

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.220
been plummeting, much
faster than Moore's law,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.900
and the volume of
genomic information

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.030
is exploding, as you
can see in this slide.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.360
This trend is not likely
to slow down anytime soon.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.600
Even reasonable
projections estimate

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.680
that the amount of genomic
data will double every year,

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.350
and will hit one exabyte
within the next five years.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.900
If the current trends persist,
we will hit a billion genomes

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.500
by 2025.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.210
That's billion with a B.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.740
But having all that
raw data is not useful

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.990
unless you're able to extract
clinical meaning from it.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.925
We will need to layer
on this raw data

00:01:05.925 --> 00:01:10.200
a variety of analytics,
ranging from data mining,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.220
novel algorithms,
intuitive visualization,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.600
all the way to very
deep machine learning,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.640
to ultimately have patient
and health care impact.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.220
This kind of data
quantity transition

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.670
has been seen by Google before.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:30.170
We have seen it in search,
in video, and also email.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.000
Now, as scientists
scale up their studies,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.980
and begin to query thousands,
and soon, millions of genomes,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.820
you will need to have scalable
technologies for manipulating,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.430
analyzing, and interpreting
enormous data sets,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.020
without the time
or cost of moving

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.700
that data from place to place.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.220
And this is where Google
believes it can help.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.540
Google Genomics that is built
on the Google Cloud platform

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.650
aims to help the life science
community organize the world's

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.832
genomic information, and make
it accessible and useful.

00:02:02.832 --> 00:02:05.040
And just to give you some
perspective on Google Cloud

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.280
Platform, just a part of
Google's infrastructure,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.430
which supports YouTube,
can handle 300 hours

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.480
of video every minute.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.700
And our search,
which returns you

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.720
the results in a
quarter of a second,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.900
that search index is over
100 petabytes in size.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.250
Now let's translate
that into genomic terms.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.860
That is the equivalent of raw
data from six whole genome

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.520
sequences uploaded to
our servers every minute,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.720
and we can index and search
the equivalent of a million

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.880
whole genome
sequences currently.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.820
Now let's talk about policy, and
mounting scientific evidence.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.060
Policy-- There's
increasing recognition

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.710
on the importance
of cloud computing

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.650
for genomic data analysis, so
much so that in early March

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.070
the NIH updated its
genomic data sharing

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.070
policy to allow use of public
cloud to store and access

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.210
genomic data.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.150
In parallel, there's mounting
evidence in the life science

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.550
community that
cloud-based genomic data

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.120
analysis is the future.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.110
Recent articles in
"Nature" showcased how much

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:11.720
cheaper, faster, and flexible
cloud computing resources were,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.880
compared to local data centers.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.080
Google Cloud Platform
resources are even

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.800
cited as a key public
cloud computing

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.870
resource that is
used increasingly

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.880
by genomics researchers.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.860
Google Cloud Platform
offers both infrastructure

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.180
as a service, and
platform as a service.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.400
However, we also build
a unique genomics API

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.400
that powers many
genomic-specific features,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.920
and like a buffet,
you can choose

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.710
whether you want to
use our infrastructure

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:45.320
services, platform services, or
genomic-specific features set

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.050
that we have built.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.030
We group the key benefits
of Google Genomics

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:53.150
as Store, Process,
Explore, and Share.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.010
Let's talk about
these one by one.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.680
Store- we enable
content-aware storage.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.820
That is, we know that you are
storing A, T, Z's, and C's.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.820
And we have separate
read and variant

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:08.750
stores, that allow a variety
of different functionality.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.160
All of this data can
be accessed by a web

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.320
API that is an implementation
on the GA4GH specifications.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.279
We store many different data
sets, like 1000 Genomes,

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:22.390
Platinum Genomes, as well
as reference sequences

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:24.300
for your analysis.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.570
I will also talk soon about
a couple of large data sets

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.170
that are stored on
Google Genomics.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:31.960
Let's move on to Process.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:34.390
We allow running of data
processing pipelines

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:37.080
to analyze public or
private genomic data stored

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.470
on Google Genomics.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.510
In addition, we inked a deal
with the Broad Institute

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.130
recently to bring
the popular GATK

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:48.400
pipeline as a service in
a cloud-optimized format.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.950
Moving on to Explore.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.050
We enable tertiary analysis of
genomic data using a variety

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.340
of tools for research purposes.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.210
We enable usage of
Google-scale tools

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.110
like BigQuery, that
allow real time analysis

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.680
of massive volumes of data.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.180
Moving on to Share.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.700
We enable sharing of these data
sets painlessly, yet securely,

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.230
with fine grained access
control, two factor

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:15.610
authentication, client
keys, security at rest

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:16.810
and at transit.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.500
And security is paramount to us.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.950
We sign BAAs that
ensure our platform

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:25.060
supports our customer's HIPAA
compliance requirements.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.370
We also have a variety of
security certifications,

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.480
and some of the highest
security encryption

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.510
standards in the industry, to
ensure that your sensitive data

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:34.085
stays safe.

00:05:37.677 --> 00:05:39.760
Now let's get a little bit
into the details of how

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:41.890
Google Genomics actually works.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.282
The sequence data
from the sequencer

00:05:44.282 --> 00:05:46.490
can be stored in a variety
of different file formats,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:50.490
such as FASTQ, BAM, or
VCFs on Google Cloud.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.190
It is then copied from Google
Cloud to Google Genomics

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.400
into the read or
variant store, at which

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.900
stage we know its
genetic information.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.510
The API calls can then be made
to make a variety of queries

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.850
or analysis on this data.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.960
If you want to do further
tertiary analysis,

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:08.090
we also enable
specific data cohorts

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.484
to be exported to BigQuery
for further analysis.

00:06:11.484 --> 00:06:12.900
The interesting
thing to note here

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.280
is that we enable users of
varying levels of expertise

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.590
to access and analyze this data.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:20.820
If you're an expert
programmer, you can write code.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.410
If you're a wet
lab scientist, you

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.550
can use a web UI to write
simple SQL TypeScripts.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.060
If you're a
bioinformatic scientist,

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.590
and your favorite tool is
RStudio or iPython Notebook,

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.760
you can directly make API
calls from your tool of choice.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.800
I'd like to provide a few
examples that illustrate

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.580
these various benefits.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.330
We will start with walking
through the Autism Speaks data

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:44.130
set, and then move on to the
National Cancer Institute's

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.010
TCGA data set.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.840
Autism Speaks is one of the
largest nonprofits focusing

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.530
on autism-related research.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:54.190
As you can see, there is
overwhelming evidence now

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:58.870
that there's a genetic basis to
the autism spectrum disorders.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.900
To understand this
genetic basis,

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.630
Autism Speaks has launched
an ambitious project

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.630
to sequence 10,000
persons, ranging

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.790
from children with autism,
affected as well as unaffected

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:12.090
siblings, and parents.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:15.120
This entire data set is
going to be made available

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.920
on Google Genomics for
qualified researchers,

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.490
and when completed, will
be over a petabyte in size.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:25.560
This data set stores genomic
data, ranging from raw reads

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.020
to the variants, as
well as phenomic data,

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:31.780
such as diagnosis result,
demographic info, family info,

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.930
and a few other
pertinent metadata.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:37.510
We already have about 1,700
whole genome sequences

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.520
and associated phenomic data for
researchers to start analyzing.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.170
Autism Speaks also built a web
portal to host its database,

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:48.490
to allow interactive
querying of the data set.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:51.490
This allows
non-bioinformaticians to easily

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.829
query and explore
interesting data cohorts.

00:07:54.829 --> 00:07:56.620
Now let's talk about
a second valuable data

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.750
set, which is the National
Cancer Institute's TCGA data

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:00.580
set.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.520
Google Genomics is involved in
two of the three winning bids

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.130
for NCI's Cancer Cloud pilot.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.900
Both the open access
TCGA data, as well as

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:12.240
the controlled access data,
requiring dbGaP authorization,

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.270
will be hosted on
Google Genomics.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:19.670
And just like Autism Speaks,
ISB is developing a web research

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:24.000
portal to allow users to
explore the TCGA data set

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.490
and select pertinent
cohorts of interest,

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:29.850
and open it directly
into an iPython notebook

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.810
to continue their analysis.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.650
Switching gears,
we will now focus

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:36.960
on the Process and Explore
aspects of our platform.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.010
For analysis, we will briefly
review Broad's GATK processing

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:41.940
pipeline.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:44.850
We will then look at the
open source code examples

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.700
that we have made
available on GitHub.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.620
And we will wrap
up with BigQuery

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.420
doing powerful analysis
on genomic data.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:54.410
Let's talk about
the first example.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:56.710
Google Genomics recently
announced a partnership

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.480
with the Broad Institute
that combines their genomics

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.000
expertise with Google's
engineering expertise

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.520
to help address complex
biological questions.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.990
A key initial foray in this, is
to bring the widely used GATK

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:12.950
pipeline as a service
on Google Genomics.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.750
This enables users to not have
to worry about infrastructure,

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:18.390
or platform, or
license, and simply

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:21.550
focus on running that
pipeline on their data.

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:23.830
Now let's talk about
BigQuery, which

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.860
was a powerful Google-scale
tool built originally

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.000
to interrogate very large
internet log files, containing

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.600
billions of entries.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:33.310
Turns out, this is
very well suited

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:36.740
for querying genomic data
for tertiary analysis needs.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:38.830
In this block,
BigQuery is being shown

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:43.300
to do a count and a plot of
all the variants with single R2

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.280
alleles on the 1000
Genome data set.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.310
As you can imagine, this is a
fairly heavy-duty calculation,

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:52.530
and this query processes
a terabyte of data,

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.960
and provides a result
in 7.4 seconds.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.110
This type of rapid
querying was not

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.090
previously feasible
without using

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:02.580
such powerful
Google-scale tools.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.630
This makes research interactive
and real-time, resulting

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:08.950
in accelerated innovation.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.420
Finally, Google and
the research community

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:14.870
are building a growing
repository of open source codes

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:18.450
and tools that are available
on GitHub for researchers.

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:20.580
They can use it for
their own pipelines

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.840
or modify it for their analysis.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.270
Listed here is a subset of
some of these tools, which

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:28.350
you will find valuable, and
I'll show two of these examples

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:29.430
now for you.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:32.350
Shown here is an example of
principal coordinate analysis

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:35.120
code that we have made
available on GitHub.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:38.960
When we run this PCA analysis on
the entire 1000 Genome data set

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.640
and color code the
resulting scatter

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.610
plot by population type,
we get a nice clustering

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.200
by ethnicity as expected.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:50.760
Here's an example of code
created by Stanford University

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.250
researchers, also
available on GitHub.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.160
This is used to perform quality
control on genomic data sets.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:00.430
This code specifically
plots heterozygocity rate

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.000
on the X chromosome
that helps easily

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.030
identify mislabeled
samples, shown

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:09.670
in blue dots towards
the top of the graph.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:12.970
We hope to continuously
improve this code set

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.990
and increase the
ecosystem of valuable code

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:19.220
that users can pull
from on our GitHub page.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.210
Like the Autism Speaks
and the TCGA data set,

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:24.240
we welcome interest
from the community

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:27.490
to host valuable data
sets on Google Genomics.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:31.470
Similarly, like GATK, we
welcome your analytics pipelines

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:34.090
to be hosted and shared on
Google Genomics as well.

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.930
And that brings us to the
conclusion of this webinar.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:39.400
We hope that you have a
good understanding of Google

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.720
Genomics platform,
and how it can really

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:44.620
help you store,
process, explore,

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:46.510
and share genomic information.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:50.110
For more information, please
visit our website listed here.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:52.060
Thank you so much.

